      <p class="eyebrow">Overview</p>
      <h2 id="abstract-title" class="section-heading">Abstract</h2>
    </div>
    <p class="abstract-copy">
      Artificial agents can solve increasingly complex tasks, yet most remain fixed after deployment and adapt unevenly when environments, requirements, or available knowledge change. MUSE frames this limitation through a five-stage spectrum of agent evolution spanning evolutionary autonomy, supervision autonomy, and optimization scope. As an integrative Stage-4 prototype, MUSE advances beyond task-conditioned partial evolution through three coordinated mechanisms. Metacognitive evolutionary gating monitors longitudinal capability evidence, decides when evolution is warranted, diagnoses capability gaps, and routes optimization demands. Interaction-grounded self-supervision derives supplementary tasks and rewards from validated environmental interactions. Holistic unison evolution then refines and synchronizes the agent's parametric core and symbolic assets under a shared evolutionary objective and boundary constraints. Together, these mechanisms turn deployment interactions into grounded supervision and coordinated capability updates while preserving useful legacy behavior.
    </p>
